# diff --git a/IR-SCIENCE.md b/IR-SCIENCE.md
# index 79f94c8..cea8647 100644
# --- a/IR-SCIENCE.md
# +++ b/IR-SCIENCE.md
# @@ -1113,6 +1113,330 @@ a picture of the film's PALETTE on these frames' structure, for judging the
#  app's distance from it; it is not a pipeline. The full sheet (the app beside
#  both filters, seven rows) was sent on 2026-09-18 rather than described.
#  
# +### 4b-viii. THE FILM FROM KODAK'S OWN DATA SHEETS — IT IS NOT A CLEAN ROTATION
# +
# +**Researched 2026-09-25, after a day of building and tuning Aerochrome
# +candidates without first reading what the film physically did.** 4b's mapping
# +came from practitioners' summaries; this comes from Kodak's own technical data,
# +read in full, with the published curves digitised from the PDFs' vector paths.
# +
# +**Primary documents, all read in full:**
# +
# +- KODAK AEROCHROME III Infrared Film 1443, publication AS-77 / TI-2562
# +  (2005, revised 11-05): 125px.com/docs/unsorted/kodak/EN_ti2562.pdf
# +- KODAK EKTACHROME Professional Infrared EIR Film, TI-2323 (2005):
# +  125px.com/docs/film/kodak/ti2323-Ektachrome_EIR.pdf, and the 1996/97
# +  edition at chrysis.net
# +- EIR processing, CIS-188: 125px.com/docs/techpubs/kodak/cis188.pdf
# +- KODAK Filters, B-3 (1999): 125px.com/docs/techpubs/kodak/b3akic.pdf
# +
# +**THE LAYERS, as sensitivity curves** (log sensitivity to reach density 1.0,
# +Process AR-5):
# +
# +- **Green layer (forms yellow dye):** flat from 400 to 570 nm, peak near
# +  550–560 nm, gone by 600 nm. Through the Wratten 12 it is a clean band, half
# +  power 519–577 nm.
# +- **Red layer (forms magenta dye):** a blue lobe, then a sharp peak at 650 nm,
# +  gone by 690 nm. Half power 628–664 nm through the filter. It sees essentially
# +  no infrared.
# +- **Infrared layer (forms cyan dye):** the strongest blue response of the
# +  three, then a steady rise through the red to a broad maximum near 730 nm,
# +  half power at 850 nm, gone by about 900 nm (Kodak: sensitive "to
# +  approximately 900 nm").
# +- **Wratten 12:** about 2% at 500 nm, 50% at 517 nm, flat near 75% from 540
# +  to 890 nm.
# +
# +**WHAT 4b LEFT OUT, AND IT MATTERS FOR ANY EMULATION.** Through the Wratten 12
# +the infrared layer is at half its peak or more from about 533 nm all the way to
# +849 nm. It is a broad red-and-infrared layer, not an infrared one. Computed from
# +the curves for a grey card in clear daylight: **only about 53% of that layer's
# +exposure is infrared (700–900 nm); about 28% is visible red and 16% visible
# +green.** The red layer is about 87% red, the green layer about 99% green. So the
# +film's red output is not "infrared" — it is mostly infrared with a large share
# +of the visible, and the three-way rotation is an idealisation of it.
# +
# +**AND THE INFRARED LAYER IS SLOW ON PURPOSE.** From the characteristic curves
# +the infrared layer needs 0.2–0.3 log more daylight exposure than the other two
# +to reach density 1.0; computed spectrally for a grey subject it receives
# +0.41–0.43 log (about 1.4 stops) less. A 1978 Photogrammetric Engineering &
# +Remote Sensing abstract on Aerochrome Infrared 2443 (vol. 44, p. 601, read as a
# +search summary only) states that the infrared layer's daylight sensitivity is
# +deliberately made about 1½ stops less than the red and green layers'. The
# +calculation and the abstract agree independently. **The consequence is the
# +sky.**
# +
# +**THE TONE CURVES** (AS-77, AR-5, Status A): maximum densities 3.3–3.7,
# +minimum 0.12–0.15, mid-scale gradients 3.6 (infrared), 2.6 (red), 4.3 (green).
# +Density 3.0 down to 0.5 spans only 0.74–0.96 log exposure — 2.5 to 3.2 stops.
# +TI-2323 gives exposure latitude as ±½ stop. It is a high-contrast film with
# +almost no latitude. E-6 processing gives results "higher in contrast" and "more
# +saturated" than AR-5; no E-6 or C-41 curves are published.
# +
# +**THE DYES** (AS-77, preliminary data): yellow peaks at 443 nm, magenta at 551
# +nm with about 0.15 unwanted blue density, cyan at about 670 nm with 0.13 at 550
# +nm and 0.19 at 410 nm.
# +
# +**WHY FOLIAGE STAYS RED AND DOES NOT WASH OUT** (derived through the Kodak
# +curves from a PROSPECT/SAIL leaf-and-canopy spectrum, not a published figure).
# +A healthy canopy is very dark in the visible — reflectance about 0.08 at 550 nm
# +and 0.02 at 670 nm — so its red-layer and green-layer exposures sit 0.3–0.8 log
# +below a grey card, and at gradients of 2.6–4.3 that is 2–3 density units of
# +magenta and yellow dye. Because the infrared layer is slow AND partly fed by
# +visible light, foliage lands only about +0.1 log above a grey card in that
# +layer: its cyan stays near mid-scale instead of clearing. **The red of the film
# +is dense magenta and yellow dye from foliage being dark in the visible — not a
# +saturated infrared record.** Overexposed by one to one and a half stops, the
# +magenta and yellow layers climb into their toe and foliage drifts from crimson
# +toward magenta-pink and loses saturation. That is the washed-out grass, and it
# +is the film's behaviour too when overexposed; it is not a defect peculiar to a
# +digital emulation.
# +
# +**WHAT THE FILM RENDERS, in Kodak's own words** (AS-77 and TI-2323):
# +
# +- healthy deciduous trees "magenta or red"; healthy evergreens "brownish red";
# +  deciduous have "much higher infrared reflectivity" than evergreens; stressed
# +  or dying foliage "from dark red to green or even yellow"
# +- painted objects "purple or blue" against red foliage; water "very dark"; ice
# +  light; algae "pink swirls"
# +- in AR-5, a red barn "pastel-green", foliage red, the sky "blue-green"
# +- in E-6, flesh "more sallow" with "yellow lips"
# +- autumn yellow leaves photograph white, red leaves yellow
# +
# +**THE SKY, DERIVED.** Skylight through the Wratten 12 is mostly green, less red,
# +very little infrared. So the green layer is well exposed (little yellow dye),
# +the red layer moderately (some magenta), and the slow infrared layer barely at
# +all (heavy cyan): blue. Under Kodak's own balance the model gives the sky hue
# +186–193° — cyan-blue, matching "blue-green". **A sky as bright as a grey card
# +renders mid-to-dark blue (value about 0.27–0.44) because its cyan layer is
# +underexposed.** Clouds render near-white, with a slight cyan cast under Kodak's
# +balance. The film's dark sky is the slow infrared layer seeing almost nothing.
# +
# +**TWO TARGETS, NOT ONE.** The two photographs measured in 4b-iii — foliage 6–9°,
# +sky 204–217° — sit close to the model when the scan is balanced so grey reads
# +neutral (foliage 354–359°, sky 196–205°). Under Kodak's own balance with the
# +Wratten 12 the same film gives magenta-crimson foliage and a cyan-blue sky, with
# +neutrals cyan. Most Aerochrome images in circulation are E-6 or C-41,
# +colour-balanced when scanned. **Which of the two the app aims at is a look
# +choice, to be shown as renders, not settled here.**
# +
# +**FILTERS.** Kodak: the Wratten 12 is "required" (AS-77); No. 15 gives "greater
# +blue attenuation" (B-3); TI-2323's colour-shift table lists CC corrections. A
# +film shooting guide (robwalwyn.com/aerochrome, read): #12 an overall blue cast
# +with magenta foliage; #16 the most neutral, "deep natural blue" skies; #21 and
# +#22 foliage toward red and skies deep blue or cyan; #25 predominantly yellow;
# +an R72 gives monochrome magenta. The model, with filter edges approximated by
# +shifting the Wratten 12 curve: #21 gives scarlet foliage with a greenish-cyan
# +sky; #25 and #29 everything yellow-orange.
# +
# +**The digitised curves and the film model** (spectral sensitivities,
# +characteristic curves, dye densities, Wratten 12, and the script that renders a
# +reflectance spectrum through them) were kept outside the repository with the
# +session's scratch; the numbers above are the parts any later work needs.
# +
# +### 4b-ix. WHAT CAN BE DONE WITH THIS CAMERA'S ONE AXIS — THE FIELD, THE LUTS AND THE LITERATURE
# +
# +Researched 2026-09-25, from practitioners' long-form tutorials, the LUT and
# +profile products, and the published science. Read status is given where it
# +matters; many academic hosts were refused by this environment's network
# +policy, and those are named at the end.
# +
# +**THE ONE AXIS IS A VEGETATION INDEX.** Model the white-balanced red channel as
# +a visible part plus an infrared part and green as infrared alone; then the
# +difference `d = R − G` over `G` is `f × (visible/infrared − 1)`, where `f` is
# +the visible share of the red channel for this conversion. **So the chroma axis
# +this camera records (4c-iii) is a red-to-infrared ratio scaled by one camera
# +constant — the same quantity as NDVI**, the standard vegetation index of remote
# +sensing. With a 665 nm filter the visible part is roughly 665–700 nm, where
# +chlorophyll absorbs, which is a genuine narrow NDVI red; with a 720 nm filter it
# +is only the dye's chromatic response between 720 and 800 nm, a red-edge proxy.
# +Above about 800 nm the colour filter array carries no colour at all (Park and
# +Kang, "Color Restoration of RGBN Multispectral Filter Array Sensor Images Based
# +on Spectral Decomposition", Sensors 16(5):719, 2016, read), which is why green
# +and blue agree within 0.6% here. This derivation is the researcher's own and not
# +a paper's; its physics is consistent with Wang et al., "Precise Estimation of
# +NDVI with a Simple NIR Sensitive RGB Camera", Sensors 20(11):3208, 2020 (read):
# +four unknowns against three responses, so red and infrared cannot be separated
# +without restricting the spectrum.
# +
# +**THE PHYSICS OF THE MATERIALS on that axis.** Leaf reflectance rises by a
# +factor of five or more between 700 and 750 nm, and a canopy darkens the visible
# +further (Seager et al., "Vegetation's Red Edge", Astrobiology 5(3), 2005, read).
# +A clear sky's red-to-infrared ratio is roughly 3–4 by Rayleigh scattering;
# +foliage's roughly 0.1 — **a 30 to 40 times difference, which is the physical
# +content of the axis.** Clouds, snow and concrete are near spectrally flat
# +(neutral on the axis). Water absorbs strongly in the infrared (Pope and Fry,
# +Applied Optics 36:8710, 1997), so it shows only surface reflection. Haze
# +flattens the sky toward neutral. No verified numbers for asphalt, concrete or
# +soil could be read (every spectral-library host was refused).
# +
# +**WHAT CANNOT BE RECOVERED, BY ANY PROCESSING.** Wavelengths the conversion's
# +filter blocks never reach the sensor. Visible green is absent, so **the film's
# +green layer — which drives Aerochrome's BLUE output — must be synthesised**;
# +with a 720 nm filter the red layer must be too. Fredembach and Süsstrunk
# +("Colouring the Near-Infrared", CIC 2008, abstract): the differences between
# +visible and infrared follow material and atmosphere, not colour, so no global
# +map from infrared to colour exists. Limmer and Lensch ("Infrared Colorization
# +Using Deep Convolutional Neural Networks", ICMLA 2016, read): what is absent
# +from the infrared "is not recoverable".
# +
# +**THE PUBLISHED PRACTICE FOR A MISSING BAND — weather satellites, which have
# +the same problem.** GOES-16 carries no green band and synthesises one as a fixed
# +blend `G = 0.45·blue + 0.45·red + 0.10·NIR` (Bah, Gunshor and Schmit,
# +"Generation of GOES-16 True Color Imagery without a Green Band", Earth and
# +Space Science 5, 2018, abstract; coefficients confirmed in satpy's
# +`SimulatedGreen`, read). Himawari's green misses the vegetation peak and blends
# +in infrared (Miller et al., BAMS 97, 2016). **satpy's `NDVIHybridGreen` makes
# +the blend weight a function of each pixel's vegetation index** (read, from the
# +source). That is this camera's situation solved operationally: a missing band
# +built per pixel from what was measured, driven by the one index the sensor
# +does measure. The other published route is a colour lookup learned from
# +registered reference images (Hogervorst and Toet, "Fast natural color mapping
# +for night-time imagery", Information Fusion 11(2), 2010, abstract), which works
# +for any target palette, including a film reference.
# +
# +**THE PRINCIPLED RENDER, AS THE LITERATURE ASSEMBLES IT** (the researcher's
# +synthesis of the sources above, not one paper's method):
# +
# +1. Take the two measured quantities: infrared luminance (clean, high signal) and
# +   the vegetation index `d/L`.
# +2. Synthesise the film's three layer exposures from them — the infrared layer
# +   mostly from luminance, the red layer from the index, the green layer as a
# +   function of the index as satellites build a missing green — fitted from
# +   reflectance spectra through the Kodak curves of 4b-viii, or from registered
# +   reference frames.
# +3. Run the exposures through the film's characteristic curves and dyes, so the
# +   dark sky, the white clouds and red-not-white foliage come out of the film's
# +   own behaviour rather than from tuning.
# +4. Estimate the colour smoothly at low resolution and re-attach it to the clean
# +   infrared detail with an edge-aware filter — the route Limmer and Lensch
# +   take (colour predicted at low frequency, then joint-bilateral filtered with
# +   the infrared image as guide), and Son and Zhang's contrast-preserving
# +   mapping (IEEE TIP 26(11), 2017, abstract).
# +5. Where the colour signal is weak, shrink the gain rather than amplify it: Lim
# +   and Silverstein, "Spatially Varying Color Correction Matrices for Reduced
# +   Noise", HP Labs HPL-2004-99, 2004 (read) — a correlated channel pair gives a
# +   high condition number, and the remedy is a spatially varying, Wiener-type
# +   matrix.
# +
# +**NOISE, IN ONE LINE OF ARITHMETIC.** `d` carries noise about √2 times a
# +channel's; any saturation gain scales signal and noise together, so **no gain
# +changes the colour's signal-to-noise ratio** — only averaging or a prior can.
# +Denoise in raw or linear space and judge the result after the colour transform
# +(Brooks et al., "Unprocessing Images for Learned Raw Denoising", CVPR 2019,
# +read). An edge-aware denoiser does not commute with a gain, so its thresholds
# +have to be scaled by the gain that follows it.
# +
# +**WHAT PRACTITIONERS SAY ABOUT 665 AND 720 NM** (all read unless marked):
# +
# +- LifePixel: Aerochrome from a 720 nm camera works "to a lesser degree"; the
# +  filter guide says at 720 nm "only the blue sky effect is possible".
# +- Kolari: 550 nm is the conversion "inspired by false color IR film"; 720 nm
# +  gives "mild color". Kolari's LUT pack by Pierre-Louis Ferrer has pink and
# +  gold foliage looks for 550–665 nm only, and no pink or red-foliage LUT for
# +  695–720 nm.
# +- David Kennard's test of 23 filters on a full-spectrum body
# +  (davidkennardphotography.com): red foliage and blue sky together appear only
# +  at 580 nm and below; at 650–720 nm a swap or hue rotation gives pink with
# +  teal, or orange with blue.
# +- Cuchara Valley Landscapes: a 720 nm capture "may not work as well".
# +- **Why, in the researcher's own reasoning, consistent with Kennard and with
# +  4c-i's solve:** when green equals blue after white balance, every pixel sits
# +  on one line through grey, so any global linear operation — swap, mixer, or a
# +  LUT that is a mixer — puts the two ends of the scene at nearly complementary
# +  hues. The film's foliage and sky (4b-iii) are 162° apart, not 180°. Both at
# +  once needs a hue-selective step or a mask, and practitioners name the costs:
# +  noise, blotching, halos at edges.
# +
# +**THE ONLY ROUTE TO THE FILM'S REAL MAPPING FOR THIS CAMERA IS TWO IMAGES.**
# +An infrared frame (this camera) and an ordinary colour frame of the same scene,
# +aligned, mapped infrared to red, visible red to green, visible green to blue.
# +Documented by JW Wong (flickr.com/photos/jw_wong, read), and it is how Kodak's
# +own digital colour-infrared cameras (DCS 420CIR and 460CIR, patent US6292212)
# +worked with a full-spectrum sensor. Hidden Realms (hiddenrealms.ch, read)
# +describes the same with an 830 nm frame and a hot-mirror frame. Costs: a
# +tripod, and ghosting on anything that moves. Channel subtraction in these
# +methods must be done in linear light (JW Wong; a linear mixer on gamma data
# +"didn't work as well"). Joshua Bird's trichrome (three filtered exposures)
# +is the same idea on film (joshuabird.com, read).
# +
# +**THE OPTICAL ROUTES NEED A FULL-SPECTRUM BODY and cannot apply here.** Kolari
# +IR Chrome and IR Chrome Lite, Candy Chrome, Midwest Optical's triple band-pass,
# +and yellow or 550 nm filters with channel arithmetic all require a
# +full-spectrum or two-spectrum camera; Kolari says IR Chrome will not work on a
# +590 nm camera or stacked with an IR filter. Hidden Realms, the most rigorous
# +source, is openly critical even of IR Chrome: foliage orange-red rather than
# +red, colours too uniform, red objects staying red where the film turns them
# +yellow.
# +
# +**LUTS AND PROFILES — WHAT EACH EXPECTS AS INPUT.** No LUT, free or paid, claims
# +to turn a 665 or 720 nm file into Aerochrome.
# +
# +- **Built for infrared files like this one:** Rob Shea's free Color Infrared
# +  LUTs v2 (470–720 nm; MIT licence, copyright 2024 Robert C. Shea, so it may be
# +  bundled with the notice kept), Rob Shea's paid PRO LUTs and Lightroom
# +  swap profiles (which name 665 and 720 nm), Kolari's False Color actions
# +  (including "Only Blue" for 720 nm), Kolari/Ferrer's pack, and PictureFX
# +  "Channel Swap for IR" (claims 550–720 nm; its author owns no IR filter).
# +  **Every one of them is a channel swap, sometimes with a curve, and every one
# +  expects the white-balanced frame BEFORE any swap.** The app applies an
# +  imported LUT last, over the look — so on a swapping look, Rob Shea's
# +  "IR RB Swap" undoes the swap outright (measured: that cube is an exact R⇄B
# +  swap). That is a real interaction for the app's LUT feature, recorded here.
# +- **Built for a full-spectrum capture with a filter:** Kolari's IR Chrome and
# +  Candy Chrome LUTs; irlab.uk's "Aerochrome 1443" and "EIR" presets (irlab.uk,
# +  a browser tool that overlaps this app, read).
# +- **Built for ordinary colour photos (faux infrared):** PictureFX Kodak
# +  Aerochrome V1.0 (marcrphoto.wordpress.com; its site states CC BY-SA 4.0 on
# +  that post, CC BY-NC 4.0 elsewhere, and reserves all rights in its footer —
# +  bundling needs the author's written permission), RNI Aerochrome, Sickboat,
# +  Kurosu, Envato and Run N Gun AeroLux packs, VSCO IR presets, Nik's Infrared
# +  Film. **Probed on test colours, PictureFX maps mid-grey to mauve and white to
# +  pink**, so on a near-neutral infrared frame it casts the whole image magenta.
# +
# +**THE SKY, HOW PRACTITIONERS AND THE LITERATURE HANDLE IT.** Practitioners move
# +the Cyan band toward blue (LifePixel), use HSL aqua and blue (Cuchara), or
# +select the sky with channel luminosity masks — the blue channel selects sky and
# +clouds, masks sharpened by multiplying the channel by itself (Pierre-Louis
# +Ferrer at Kolari, read). Lightroom users diagnose a sky mask by dropping
# +exposure hard and build a Color Range mask from a dragged box (Fstoppers,
# +read). darktable's masks carry a "feathering guide" that aligns a mask's edge
# +to the image's edges before blurring (docs.darktable.org, read). Several
# +sources say swaps fringe where foliage meets sky; the remedies named are
# +optical, apertures around f/5.6–f/8, and the raw converter's chromatic
# +aberration tools.
# +
# +**THE SKY SELECTION'S HALO, AND THE PUBLISHED FIX.** Liba et al., "Sky
# +Optimization: Semantically aware image processing of skies in low-light
# +photography", CVPR Workshops 2020, arXiv 2006.10172 (method read): the sky mask
# +is inferred at low resolution and refined by a **weighted** guided filter, with
# +each pixel weighted by a confidence that falls to about 0.01 in the uncertain
# +band around the boundary, so uncertain pixels are filled from confident
# +neighbours through the local linear model. **It needs a large window: a
# +support of 64 was right, 16 missed bridge cables.** Their user study found
# +binary masks produce worse sky edits. Lischinski et al., "Interactive Local
# +Adjustment of Tonal Values", SIGGRAPH 2006 (read, partial): propagate the
# +ADJUSTMENT as an edge-aware field, rather than blending two renders across a
# +soft mask; a hand-made mask must follow the boundary closely and be carefully
# +feathered to avoid artefacts. Hessel and Morel (arXiv 1808.09411, read): a halo
# +is an edge smoothed where it should have been preserved, and the plain guided
# +filter is among the filters that produce one. **This app's `refineSkyMask`
# +uses the plain filter with a window of 12 at 1024 px and gives every pixel equal
# +weight** — including the coarse bitmap's soft boundary it exists to correct,
# +which is where the measured halo sits (NIR_3466: selection 29% at 10 px from a
# +building, 90% only at 160 px). The weighted filter with a large support is the
# +published remedy, and it is the first thing to build.
# +
# +**SOURCES THIS ENVIRONMENT REFUSED**, for a later session with more access:
# +the spectral libraries (pubs.usgs.gov, speclib.jpl.nasa.gov,
# +ecostress.jpl.nasa.gov), hal.science (Rabatel, Gorretta and Labbé 2014, the
# +closest paper to this camera: simulating a band from a single converted
# +camera's filtered sensitivities), mdpi.com (Fent and Meldrum 2016, the only
# +paper targeting EIR hues directly, by a Foveon sensor and a green-pass filter),
# +www.asprs.org (Fritz 1967 on the film's sensitometry), truecolorinfrared.com
# +(a measured IR Chrome transmission curve), freshluts.com, gumroad.com,
# +kurosu.co, www.dropbox.com (dl.dropboxusercontent.com worked), and
# +www.plferrer.photos.
# +
#  ## 4c. THE CRUX IS NIR CONTAMINATION, AND A ROTATION ALONE CANNOT FIX IT
#  
#  **Researched 2026-09-16, after shipping the rotation bare and reporting that it
